th1-surjection-curation-001+math1-bijection-curation-001+math1-union-curation-001+math1-proper-curation-001+math1-complement-curation-001+math1-complement-render-001+math1-division-curation-001+math1-reciprocal-curation-001+math1-inequality-curation-001+math1-inequality-feedback-001+math1-maximum-curation-001+math1-minimum-curation-001+math1-boundedset-curation-001+math1-endpoint-curation-001+audit-groups-20261027+topup-math-i-20261028+topup-math-ii-20261028+topup-pt-20261028+topup-math-iii-20261028","concept_id":"Year-1/1a-Math-I-Calculus/squeeze-theorem-for-sequences.html","concept":{"concept_id":"Year-1/1a-Math-I-Calculus/squeeze-theorem-for-sequences.html","questions":[{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_squeeze_theorem_for_sequences_ff03df82_001","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What does the squeeze theorem for sequences conclude from $a_n\\leq b_n\\leq c_n$ and $a_n\\to L$, $c_n\\to L$?","options":["$b_n = L$ for every $n$","$b_n$ must be monotone","$a_n = c_n$ for every $n$","$b_n\\to L$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"8e48dc78583627733789c961","time_limit":25},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_squeeze_theorem_for_sequences_ff03df82_002","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Which condition is not required by the squeeze theorem for sequences?","options":["The inequalities $a_n\\leq b_n\\leq c_n$","Convergence of $(a_n)$","Convergence of $(c_n)$ to the same limit as $(a_n)$","Monotonicity of $(b_n)$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"823a95233a31a549b1ee1c89","time_limit":25},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_squeeze_theorem_for_sequences_ff03df82_003","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"If $-\\frac{1}{n}\\leq b_n\\leq \\frac{1}{n}$ for all $n\\geq 1$, what limit follows?","options":["$b_n\\to 1$","$b_n\\to 0$","$b_n\\to -1$","The sequence $(b_n)$ must diverge."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"b295dac92ceb39453e18e5f3","time_limit":20},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_squeeze_theorem_for_sequences_ff03df82_008","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"The limit $\\displaystyle\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}n\\sin\\!\\left(\\frac{1}{n}\\right)=1$ is established by which type of argument?","options":["A product rule: the limit of $n$ times the limit of $\\sin(1/n)$","Direct substitution of $n=\\infty$ into $n\\sin(1/n)$","A single upper bound $n\\sin(1/n)\\leq n$ from $\\sin t\\leq1$","A bounding argument via the squeeze theorem"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"386125705c82985497b453d3","time_limit":30},{"id":"squeeze_theorem_for_sequences_new_018","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"What is $\\displaystyle\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\sqrt[n]{n^2+n+1}$?","options":["$+\\infty$","$\\mathrm{e}$","$3$","$1$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"0be89efe26ccbf8293fd0277","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_squeeze_theorem_for_sequences_ff03df82_006_curated20261027_001","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Let $a_n\\to L$ and $c_n\\to L$. Which condition on the chain $a_n\\le b_n\\le c_n$ is enough to conclude $b_n\\to L$ by the squeeze theorem?","options":["It holds for the first index $n=1$","It holds for every even $n$, with no condition for odd $n$","It holds for every $n$ from some index $N$ on","It holds once $(b_n)$ is known to converge"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"577fd765e0a5cc6ed1b5362f","time_limit":35},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_squeeze_theorem_for_sequences_ff03df82_009_curated20261027_002","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Suppose $a_n\\le b_n\\le c_n$ for every $n$, $a_n\\to L$ and $c_n\\to M$ with $L\\neq M$. What can the squeeze theorem for sequences conclude about $(b_n)$?","options":["$b_n\\to L$, inheriting the limit of the lower bound","$b_n\\to M$, inheriting the limit of the upper bound","$b_n\\to\\tfrac{L+M}{2}$, the midpoint of the outer limits","No limit is forced, because the theorem needs $L=M$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"5a3b33d5ab7745369df2a538","time_limit":35},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_squeeze_theorem_for_sequences_ff03df82_012_curated20261027_003","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Assume $a_n\\le b_n\\le c_n$ for every $n$, $a_n\\to0$, and $(c_n)$ is bounded. Do these assumptions force $b_n\\to0$?","options":["Yes, since boundedness of $(c_n)$ is enough","Yes, provided $(c_n)$ is also monotone","No, and in fact $(b_n)$ must diverge","No, convergence of $(b_n)$ is not forced"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"cdf4165583eca5e3474a66f0","time_limit":40},{"id":"squeeze_theorem_for_sequences_new_016_curated20261027_004","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Suppose $|b_n-3|\\le\\dfrac{1}{n}$ for all $n\\ge1$. What follows?","options":["$b_n\\to3$","$b_n\\to0$, since $\\frac{1}{n}\\to0$","Nothing, since $b_n$ may alternate above and below $3$","$b_n\\to3$ only if $b_n\\ge3$ for all large $n$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"ef31388bf3b8886f0c364e12","time_limit":30}]}}